00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:41.760
Who are you?

264
00:09:41.900 --> 00:09:42.340
You're right.

265
00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:43.140
What am I saying?

266
00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:44.600
I want the fast fact.

267
00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:45.120
Of course.

268
00:09:45.420 --> 00:09:46.540
Finish fast facts.

269
00:09:46.580 --> 00:09:47.860
And then go to fan questions.

270
00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:48.660
I'm sorry.

271
00:09:48.860 --> 00:09:51.440
I'm feeling very loosey-goosey today, obviously.

272
00:09:51.660 --> 00:09:52.060
All right.

273
00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:52.340
Oh, my gosh.

274
00:09:52.480 --> 00:09:53.800
Final fast fact.

275
00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:57.080
In this episode, we introduce two new characters.

276
00:09:57.380 --> 00:09:58.380
Yes, we do.

277
00:09:58.380 --> 00:10:05.980
Toby Flindersen and Kelly Kapoor, played by two more members of our writing staff, Paul Lieberstein

278
00:10:05.980 --> 00:10:07.200
and Mindy Kaling.

279
00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:07.780
Yes.

280
00:10:07.880 --> 00:10:10.700
I do have to say, I love the name Toby Flindersen.

281
00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:15.500
I love when a character's name sort of describes the character.

282
00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:19.760
But like the name Toby Flindersen, like when you see Paul on screen as Toby, you're like,

283
00:10:19.860 --> 00:10:21.400
oh, of course that's Toby Flindersen.

284
00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:22.140
That's who that is.

285
00:10:22.140 --> 00:10:23.020
Absolutely perfect.

286
00:10:23.360 --> 00:10:27.480
A lot of people ask, why were so many writers, actors on the show?

287
00:10:27.640 --> 00:10:29.740
And that was Greg Daniels.

288
00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:35.580
Greg believed that it would be a good idea for writers to have the experience of acting

289
00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:41.360
because it would give them a deeper appreciation of what an actor does, which I have to say

290
00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:42.280
is so brilliant.

291
00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:49.840
And I think there are a lot of directors that I know go take acting classes just so they

292
00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:52.440
can better speak to actors and understand actors.

293
00:10:52.520 --> 00:10:57.480
And it completely worked on our show that the writers were immersed with us because they

294
00:10:57.480 --> 00:11:03.260
really got to not just understand our jobs, but they got to see us being our characters

295
00:11:03.260 --> 00:11:03.800
firsthand.

296
00:11:04.100 --> 00:11:08.920
They got to see our little improvisations that they would never see if they were just stuck

297
00:11:08.920 --> 00:11:09.840
up in the writer's room.

298
00:11:09.900 --> 00:11:14.040
And all of that ended up becoming a part of the show.

299
00:11:14.240 --> 00:11:15.420
I think it was so great.

300
00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:19.740
It really made us a cohesive show between the two departments, sort of the writers,

301
00:11:19.840 --> 00:11:21.740
the actors, this creative partnership.

302
00:11:21.740 --> 00:11:26.220
It always felt like a partnership to me and made me feel safe like I could try things.

303
00:11:26.420 --> 00:11:26.740
Yes.

304
00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:30.300
But BJ and Mindy were really great performers.

305
00:11:30.520 --> 00:11:31.440
They were crossovers.

306
00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:33.680
But Paul was not a performer.

307
00:11:33.900 --> 00:11:35.840
Paul had only just been a writer.

308
00:11:36.020 --> 00:11:39.980
So I know this was a huge step out of his comfort zone.

309
00:11:40.140 --> 00:11:45.180
And I think he thought it was going to be just this one little line in this episode.

310
00:11:45.380 --> 00:11:45.480
Right.

311
00:11:45.480 --> 00:11:47.080
Well, Jenna texted him this morning.

312
00:11:47.100 --> 00:11:47.440
I did.

313
00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:50.600
And was like, Paul, tell us the dish on how you became Toby.

314
00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:53.580
And he just said, Greg kind of made me do it.

315
00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:59.100
He said he remembered walking in one day and it had been decided that he was going to play

316
00:11:59.100 --> 00:12:00.420
this role of Toby.

317
00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:04.140
And he really thought it was just going to be this one line.

318
00:12:04.280 --> 00:12:04.460
Right.

319
00:12:04.460 --> 00:12:05.600
He didn't think he was going to be-

320
00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:06.540
A series regular.

321
00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:10.000
No, he did not think it was going to grow into what it was.

322
00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:14.420
But Kevin Reilly, who was the head of NBC at the time, saw this episode.

323
00:12:14.620 --> 00:12:15.360
He loved it.

324
00:12:15.480 --> 00:12:19.340
He loved the dynamic between Michael Scott and Toby Flunderson.

325
00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:21.260
And he said, I want more of that.

326
00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:22.240
I want more Toby.

327
00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:26.960
And so then Paul officially became an actor on the show.

328
00:12:26.960 --> 00:12:30.980
Paul, who was not a performer and who was kind of a quiet person in real life, all of a

329
00:12:30.980 --> 00:12:34.180
sudden became a full-time actor and writer.

330
00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:40.440
I think something we have to talk about, too, is the transformation of Kelly Kapoor.

331
00:12:40.700 --> 00:12:43.860
A lot of fans wrote in asking about this.

332
00:12:43.960 --> 00:12:46.400
And I'm sure you've got a note card about it, Angela.

333
00:12:46.600 --> 00:12:47.160
I do.

334
00:12:47.380 --> 00:12:49.260
First of all, all my note cards today are green.

335
00:12:49.440 --> 00:12:51.000
And Jenna has already said they're whorish.

336
00:12:51.140 --> 00:12:52.420
But whatever, Jenna.

337
00:12:52.700 --> 00:12:56.680
So I have a note card that's just written, Kelly's outfit.

338
00:12:56.920 --> 00:12:57.220
Yeah.

339
00:12:57.460 --> 00:12:58.460
Exclamation point.

340
00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:00.240
Because this is hilarious.

341
00:13:00.240 --> 00:13:05.300
Anyone that knows Mindy knows that after this episode, she was like, yeah, I'm not

342
00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:06.580
going to dress like that, okay?

343
00:13:07.140 --> 00:13:10.360
Because she had on this sort of paisley blouse and those pants.

344
00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:11.820
That went all the way up to her neck.

345
00:13:11.920 --> 00:13:12.300
I know.

346
00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:14.000
And her hair up in an updo.

347
00:13:14.220 --> 00:13:15.140
And it was just so funny to me.

348
00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:15.860
Very fussy.

349
00:13:16.020 --> 00:13:19.260
Very fussy and sort of just buttoned up.

350
00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:23.720
And right after this episode, I feel like she was like, yeah, I'm going to come in for

351
00:13:23.720 --> 00:13:24.880
a real wardrobe fitting.

352
00:13:24.880 --> 00:13:28.180
And we're going to change up the way Kelly Kapoor looks.

353
00:13:28.180 --> 00:13:33.320
I think when she realized, same as Paul, that this was going to be more than just a one-off,

354
00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:38.600
that she would be appearing regularly on the show, not only did her wardrobe change,

355
00:13:38.720 --> 00:13:41.180
but the whole conceit of her character change.

356
00:13:41.180 --> 00:13:41.440
Yeah.

357
00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:47.440
And she started writing herself more into the character, the love of Beyonce and, you know,

358
00:13:47.500 --> 00:13:50.860
the clothes and the fashion show, fashion show, fashion show at lunch.

359
00:13:50.860 --> 00:13:51.700
And then-

360
00:13:51.700 --> 00:13:52.400
That's all Mindy.

361
00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:53.680
The romance with BJ.

362
00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:54.820
Yes, exactly.

363
00:13:55.780 --> 00:13:55.830
Correct.

364
00:13:57.500 --> 00:14:00.400
All of those elements sort of weaved their way into the show.

365
00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:01.360
But it is true.

366
00:14:01.360 --> 00:14:05.040
In this first incarnation of Kelly Kapoor, she is-

367
00:14:05.040 --> 00:14:05.760
Very different.

368
00:14:05.880 --> 00:14:09.020
Not the Kelly we eventually learned to love.

369
00:14:09.120 --> 00:14:13.800
And I love the Kelly she became, though, because she was so fun and just added so much,

370
00:14:13.800 --> 00:14:17.880
like, sort of like, just sunshine to, like, whatever scene she was in.

371
00:14:17.880 --> 00:14:20.880
And I don't know how to say it, but, like, there was always- she was just all so bubbly

372
00:14:20.880 --> 00:14:23.380
and everything, and everyone else was so drab.

373
00:14:23.480 --> 00:14:25.780
So I thought she added much-needed energy.

374
00:14:25.920 --> 00:14:27.300
She added literal color.

375
00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:27.780
Yeah, she did.

376
00:14:27.780 --> 00:14:30.420
She was the only person who dressed literally colorfully.

377
00:14:31.640 --> 00:14:33.400
So do you want my fan question?

378
00:14:33.920 --> 00:14:34.680
Do I?

379
00:14:34.880 --> 00:14:35.120
Okay.

380
00:14:35.780 --> 00:14:37.740
Heather from Twitter asked-

381
00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:38.340
Hi, Heather.

382
00:14:38.500 --> 00:14:38.900
Hi, Heather.

383
00:14:39.540 --> 00:14:44.660
Heather asked, since it was only the second episode, what was the atmosphere on set like

384
00:14:44.660 --> 00:14:45.080
filming?

385
00:14:46.120 --> 00:14:52.360
And I thought that was a really good question, because we shot Diversity Day about six months

386
00:14:52.360 --> 00:14:53.540
after we shot the pilot.

387
00:14:53.680 --> 00:14:57.800
We took this really long break, because we were waiting to find out if we were going to

388
00:14:57.800 --> 00:14:58.960
get picked up or not.

389
00:15:01.260 --> 00:15:04.940
And it was so thrilling to be reunited again.

390
00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:08.700
I remember when we were watching Diversity Day, people have asked me, when did you feel

391
00:15:08.700 --> 00:15:09.460
like you had a hit?

392
00:15:09.460 --> 00:15:14.280
And I said, it's the moment we were all in the room, and we all had those note cards taped

393
00:15:14.280 --> 00:15:15.200
to our foreheads.

394
00:15:15.940 --> 00:15:20.560
And I was just looking around at all of us, and Steve was being Michael, and he was being

395
00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:21.440
so hilarious.

396
00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:26.320
And I thought, oh, my God, if we can just get anyone to watch the show, we're going to

397
00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:26.700
be a hit.

398
00:15:26.700 --> 00:15:29.100
If anyone sees it, it's so good.

399
00:15:29.360 --> 00:15:34.520
And it really was in that moment, I was like, oh, wow, this is lightning in a bottle.

400
00:15:35.040 --> 00:15:36.740
Now, how do we get the world to watch it?

401
00:15:37.000 --> 00:15:38.720
Aw, I love that, Ange.

402
00:15:39.400 --> 00:15:40.620
Listen, should we take a break?

403
00:15:40.920 --> 00:15:42.000
Yeah, let's take a break.

404
00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:43.800
I have lots of note cards.

405
00:15:43.940 --> 00:15:44.300
Well, perfect.

406
00:15:44.300 --> 00:15:48.160
When we come back, we will really break down Diversity Day and get into the scenes and tell

407
00:15:48.160 --> 00:15:48.980
you all our stories.

408
00:15:49.400 --> 00:15:50.620
You say diversity.

409
00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:52.280
I say diversity.

410
00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:53.360
What?

411
00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:54.680
Yeah, it's just been bugging me.

412
00:15:54.800 --> 00:15:55.040
But anyway.

413
00:15:55.560 --> 00:15:55.610
Diversity?

414
00:15:55.880 --> 00:15:57.060
Okay, let's go to a break.

415
00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:05.360
And we're back.

416
00:16:05.580 --> 00:16:05.980
Hello.

417
00:16:06.480 --> 00:16:11.280
All right, so the Diversity Day episode starts with Michael explaining that today, the

418
00:16:11.280 --> 00:16:14.320
office is having a mandatory diversity training.

419
00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:14.920
Yes.

420
00:16:15.080 --> 00:16:19.460
We also learned that today is the day that Jim is going to make his biggest sale of the

421
00:16:19.460 --> 00:16:19.740
year.

422
00:16:19.880 --> 00:16:24.800
He explains he only has to make one phone call, and it amounts to 25% of his total sales

423
00:16:24.800 --> 00:16:25.360
for the year.

424
00:16:25.560 --> 00:16:29.560
But Dwight keeps making it impossible for him to make that call.

425
00:16:29.880 --> 00:16:34.960
And so the first way that Dwight makes it impossible for him to make this phone call is that he must

426
00:16:34.960 --> 00:16:36.960
shred paper at his desk.

427
00:16:37.060 --> 00:16:38.000
Oh, Dwight.

428
00:16:38.000 --> 00:16:42.720
Which I always thought was funny because, you know, Pam has that industrial-sized shredder

429
00:16:42.720 --> 00:16:43.760
behind her desk.

430
00:16:43.880 --> 00:16:47.900
Pam has a shredder like the size of a Xerox machine behind her desk.

431
00:16:47.940 --> 00:16:49.280
Which we never use.

432
00:16:49.280 --> 00:16:49.740
Never use.

433
00:16:49.820 --> 00:16:50.040
Ever.

434
00:16:50.140 --> 00:16:51.880
I never shredded a single thing in it.

435
00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:54.740
And it used to get in the way when the camera—

436
00:16:54.740 --> 00:16:55.600
You have to move it.

437
00:16:55.740 --> 00:16:59.540
And it was a big argument over whether or not they could move it.

438
00:16:59.620 --> 00:17:05.200
Like, the integrity of the documentary crew, would they have moved the industrial shredder

439
00:17:05.200 --> 00:17:05.840
or not?

440
00:17:05.840 --> 00:17:10.920
And so I remember a day we were on set, and they wanted to get this shot behind my desk.

441
00:17:11.420 --> 00:17:16.720
And they said, if we could just move the shredder, we could get the shot you're asking

442
00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:16.980
for.

443
00:17:17.060 --> 00:17:21.700
And it was like a 30-minute argument over whether or not they could move or not move

444
00:17:21.700 --> 00:17:22.300
the shredder.

445
00:17:22.360 --> 00:17:22.560
Right.

446
00:17:22.560 --> 00:17:25.340
Because they didn't want to, like, ruin the integrity of the documentary look.

447
00:17:25.340 --> 00:17:25.680
Yes.

448
00:17:25.720 --> 00:17:27.360
This was a big thing all the time.

449
00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:30.800
They decided, you know, you can't move walls.

450
00:17:30.820 --> 00:17:31.660
You can't move windows.

451
00:17:31.700 --> 00:17:33.160
You can't move furniture.

452
00:17:33.160 --> 00:17:37.680
They did agree you could move this one plant around, the ficus.

453
00:17:37.800 --> 00:17:38.880
You could move the ficus.

454
00:17:38.880 --> 00:17:43.520
They clearly agreed that you could move that conference table out and put chairs, and then

455
00:17:43.520 --> 00:17:44.800
all of a sudden the conference table would be back.

456
00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:45.660
I've had—

457
00:17:45.660 --> 00:17:47.240
Where did the conference table go?

458
00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:47.460
Where did the conference table go?

459
00:17:47.620 --> 00:17:48.140
I know.

460
00:17:48.260 --> 00:17:52.760
I've had so many people write to me, like, on social media be like, what happened with

461
00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:53.480
the conference table?

462
00:17:53.560 --> 00:17:54.640
It was there, and then it's gone.

463
00:17:54.760 --> 00:17:56.040
Where does the conference table go?

464
00:17:56.040 --> 00:18:00.120
And I told them, I said, you know what I would have loved at the end of one episode?

465
00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:06.060
All it would take is one episode and the whole nine seasons if at the end credits it

466
00:18:06.060 --> 00:18:12.480
just showed, like, Kevin and, like, Jim breaking down the conference table and Dwight and, like,

467
00:18:12.620 --> 00:18:15.980
stacking it in some storage thing and then having to go get it and put it back together.

468
00:18:16.000 --> 00:18:17.540
But just as the credits rolled.

469
00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:18.100
Yes.

470
00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:22.020
I would love to see them carrying the conference table down to the warehouse or something.

471
00:18:22.020 --> 00:18:22.280
Like, in pieces.

472
00:18:22.280 --> 00:18:23.420
Because where is it?

473
00:18:23.620 --> 00:18:24.120
I don't know.

474
00:18:24.120 --> 00:18:25.680
There is nowhere for it to be.

475
00:18:25.760 --> 00:18:26.280
I don't know.

476
00:18:26.740 --> 00:18:29.260
It is—that is one of the great mysteries of the show.

477
00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:30.460
Where is the conference table?

478
00:18:30.620 --> 00:18:33.720
Every time Michael was, like, conference room meeting, that they were like, ugh,

479
00:18:33.900 --> 00:18:35.540
because they knew they had to break down that table.

480
00:18:36.700 --> 00:18:37.380
Okay, okay.

481
00:18:37.400 --> 00:18:40.480
Well, do you remember that shredding scene where Rain is shredding the paper?

482
00:18:41.180 --> 00:18:41.580
Yes.

483
00:18:41.780 --> 00:18:43.000
And he—so he's shredding paper.

484
00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:43.700
He's shredding paper.

485
00:18:44.040 --> 00:18:50.260
So then Rain notices that there's a special slot on his personal shredder for a credit card,

486
00:18:50.280 --> 00:18:51.540
and he kind of stopped the filming.

487
00:18:51.540 --> 00:18:55.640
And he said, guys, I think I should shred a credit card.

488
00:18:56.340 --> 00:18:58.380
And we were thinking, well, okay.

489
00:18:59.020 --> 00:19:01.680
And he said, no, there's a special slot for it.

490
00:19:01.760 --> 00:19:02.020
I know.

491
00:19:02.060 --> 00:19:03.400
Everyone was like, is it going to work?

492
00:19:03.720 --> 00:19:05.720
And so he did it.

493
00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:06.340
He did it.

494
00:19:06.420 --> 00:19:10.060
He put—they got him, like, a fake, like, credit card kind of thing, the props department.

495
00:19:10.300 --> 00:19:12.560
And he did it, but it almost broke that shredder.

496
00:19:12.660 --> 00:19:13.760
It started going like—

497
00:19:15.520 --> 00:19:20.000
I remember sitting there and thinking, is it—you know, is it—what's happening?

498
00:19:20.340 --> 00:19:21.000
What's happening?

499
00:19:21.240 --> 00:19:22.440
But he was so determined.

500
00:19:22.760 --> 00:19:25.360
I also think Rain is like a gadget person a little bit.

501
00:19:25.360 --> 00:19:26.800
So he was like, oh, there's a thing on here.

502
00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:27.580
I need to do it.

503
00:19:27.700 --> 00:19:29.520
Yeah, I need to work this part of the machine.

504
00:19:29.520 --> 00:19:30.060
I need to do it.

505
00:19:30.340 --> 00:19:31.800
Angela, would you like to do a card?

506
00:19:32.000 --> 00:19:33.100
I would, Jenna Fisher.

507
00:19:33.100 --> 00:19:36.900
Okay, so this card applies to the opening shot of the whole episode.

508
00:19:37.100 --> 00:19:40.380
There is a great establishing shot of the Homer Simpson doll.

509
00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:44.220
The camera is going past Phyllis's desk, and it's sitting right there.

510
00:19:44.680 --> 00:19:49.640
And, you know, it just makes me smile because I know that Greg Daniels wrote on The Simpsons,

511
00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:54.940
and this was his little, like, nod to, like, the first part of his career, right?

512
00:19:55.120 --> 00:19:55.420
Yes.

513
00:19:55.600 --> 00:19:58.740
And it sat there all nine years, that Simpson doll.

514
00:19:58.920 --> 00:19:59.980
So I love that.

515
00:19:59.980 --> 00:20:01.760
That's just, like, kind of a dorky moment.

